      "url": "https://x.com/spotlightoncn/status/1975441647585628202",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/spotlightoncn/status/1975441647585628202",
      "text": "A Reuters investigation, based on internal documents and interviews, has revealed that the Chinese embassy in Manila paid a local marketing firm to wage a covert cyber campaign in the Philippines.\n\nThe operation was run by InfinitUs Marketing Solutions, a Manila-based, Chinese-owned company that was tasked with \"guiding public opinion\" on behalf of the embassy, according to a review of the firm's internal contracts and work-progress reports.\n\nThe primary tool of the campaign was a \"troll army\" of fake Facebook profiles created by InfinitUs to masquerade as pro-Beijing Filipino citizens, posting comments and sharing content to support China's agenda.\n\nThe campaign's objectives were to weaken domestic support for the Philippine government's maritime claims in the South China Sea and to sow discord over Manila's security alliance with the United States.\n\nInternal reports show specific examples of the troll army's work, including propagating content about the \"cons\" of the U.S. deploying Typhon missiles in the Philippines.\n\nAnother key focus of the disinformation campaign was health. The fake accounts circulated negative articles about Western-made COVID-19 vaccines while simultaneously praising China's Sinovac shot.\n\nThe operation also involved targeted harassment. The fake accounts were used to \"swarm\" the social media pages of Filipino politicians critical of China, such as then-Congressman Robert Ace Barbers, flooding their posts with hundreds of negative comments and accusations.\n\nTo amplify its message, InfinitUs created a proxy media outlet called \"Ni Hao Manila,\" which was designed to look like a legitimate Filipino-run news source but was used to post videos criticizing U.S.-Philippines security cooperation.\n\nIn a parallel effort to cultivate influence, the Chinese embassy funded cash awards which were distributed by the Association of Philippines-China Understanding (APCU), an organization with ties to the Chinese Communist Party.\n\nThese awards, worth thousands of dollars, were given to dozens of prominent Filipino officials, academics, and media personalities. A separate Philippine security agency report, seen by Reuters, had identified several of these same individuals as \"instruments for Chinese influence.\"\n\nThis covert influence campaign has been unfolding as the Philippines, under President Ferdinand Marcos Jr., strengthens its security ties with the United States—a sharp reversal from the pro-Beijing policy of his predecessor, Rodrigo Duterte.\n\nA former senior Philippine official told Reuters that the \"end goal\" of China's campaign is to make the country \"compliant\" with Beijing's interests, and the report notes that polls show the pro-Beijing former president's daughter is a frontrunner for the 2028 presidential election.\nhttps://t.co/BzqohE09OM",
